    1 => [
        'title' => 'The 11 Core Criteria of Substance Use Disorder (SUD) & Severity Stratification',
        'slug' => 'substance-use-disorder-11-criteria-severity',
        'description' => 'The unified SUD paradigm in DSM-5, elimination of the abuse vs. dependence dichotomy, 11 core criteria across 4 clusters, and severity grading.',
        'overview' => "Substance-Related and Addictive Disorders in DSM-5 underwent a profound structural transformation that discarded the historical, flawed dichotomy between 'Substance Abuse' and 'Substance Dependence'. In DSM-IV, substance abuse was conceptualized as a mild or early stage of disease defined largely by legal or social consequences, while substance dependence required physiological tolerance or withdrawal. Decades of psychometric and clinical research demonstrated that substance pathology exists along a continuous, single-dimensional severity spectrum.\n\nDSM-5 consolidated abuse and dependence into a unified, dimensional diagnosis: Substance Use Disorder (SUD). The diagnostic architecture consists of 11 universal criteria organized into four distinct functional clusters: (1) Impaired control over substance use (Criteria 1–4); (2) Social impairment (Criteria 5–7); (3) Risky use of the substance (Criteria 8–9); and (4) Pharmacological criteria (Criteria 10–11: Tolerance and Withdrawal).\n\nA critical diagnostic exception in DSM-5 addresses the pharmacological criteria: Tolerance and Withdrawal are NOT counted toward a diagnosis of SUD when an individual is taking prescribed medications under appropriate medical supervision (such as prescribed opioids for post-surgical pain or prescribed benzodiazepines for anxiety). Severity is graded strictly by the number of criteria met within a 12-month period.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "The 11 Universal DSM-5 Criteria for Substance Use Disorder (requiring at least 2 criteria met within a 12-month period):\n\nCluster 1: Impaired Control (Criteria 1–4)\n1. Taking the substance in larger amounts or over a longer period than was originally intended.\n2. Persistent desire or unsuccessful efforts to cut down or control substance use.\n3. Spending a great deal of time in activities necessary to obtain the substance, use the substance, or recover from its effects.\n4. Craving, or a strong desire or urge to use the substance (newly added to DSM-5).\n\nCluster 2: Social Impairment (Criteria 5–7)\n5. Recurrent substance use resulting in a failure to fulfill major role obligations at work, school, or home.\n6. Continued substance use despite having persistent or recurrent social or interpersonal problems caused or exacerbated by the effects of the substance.\n7. Important social, occupational, or recreational activities are given up or reduced because of substance use.\n\nCluster 3: Risky Use (Criteria 8–9)\n8. Recurrent substance use in situations in which it is physically hazardous (e.g., driving while intoxicated).\n9. Continued substance use despite knowledge of having a persistent or recurrent physical or psychological problem that is likely to have been caused or exacerbated by the substance.\n\nCluster 4: Pharmacological Criteria (Criteria 10–11)\n10. Tolerance: A need for markedly increased amounts of the substance to achieve intoxication or desired effect, or a markedly diminished effect with continued use of the same amount.\n11. Withdrawal: The characteristic withdrawal syndrome for the substance, or the substance is taken to relieve or avoid withdrawal symptoms.\n\nSeverity Grading (within a 12-month period):\n• Mild: Presence of 2 to 3 criteria.\n• Moderate: Presence of 4 to 5 criteria.\n• Severe: Presence of 6 or more criteria.",
        'specifiers' => "Remission Specifiers:\n• 'In early remission': None of the criteria for Substance Use Disorder have been met for at least 3 months but for less than 12 months (with the exception of Criterion 4, 'Craving').\n• 'In sustained remission': None of the criteria have been met at any time during a period of 12 months or longer (with the exception of 'Craving').\n\nEnvironmental Specifiers: 'In a controlled environment' (e.g., closely supervised prison, therapeutic community) or 'On maintenance therapy' (e.g., methadone, buprenorphine, nicotine patch).",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Substance Use Disorder vs. Normal Social/Recreational Use: Recreational use does not meet the 2-symptom threshold and causes no functional role impairment, craving, or hazardous consequences.\n\nPrescription Medication Use vs. SUD: In a cancer patient taking prescribed morphine as directed, tolerance and withdrawal are expected physiological adaptations and do NOT count toward SUD.\n\nDSM-IV vs. DSM-5 Criteria: DSM-5 added 'Craving' as Criterion 4 and ELIMINATED 'Recurrent substance-related legal problems' due to cultural and racial disparities in law enforcement.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 35-year-old accountant who drinks wine every evening. Over the past year, the accountant has repeatedly drunk a full bottle instead of one glass (larger amounts than intended), has tried and failed four times to quit (unsuccessful cut-down), experiences intense mid-afternoon cravings for wine (craving), arrives late to morning client meetings (role failure), and requires two bottles to feel relaxed where one previously sufficed (tolerance).\n\nMeeting 5 criteria (Criteria 1, 2, 4, 5, 10) within the past 12 months, the clinical formulation confirms Alcohol Use Disorder, Moderate Severity, establishing eligibility for medical and psychosocial intervention.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: DSM-5 eliminated the abuse vs. dependence dichotomy, creating a single dimensional Substance Use Disorder based on 11 criteria across 4 clusters. Severity: Mild = 2-3 criteria; Moderate = 4-5 criteria; Severe = 6+ criteria within a 12-month period. DSM-5 added Craving and removed Legal Problems. Tolerance and Withdrawal do NOT count if the substance is taken as prescribed under medical supervision. Early remission = 3 to 12 months; Sustained remission = >= 12 months.",
