    3 => [
        'title' => 'Alcohol-Related Disorders (Use Disorder, Intoxication, Delirium Tremens, Wernicke’s)',
        'slug' => 'alcohol-related-disorders-delirium-tremens-wernicke',
        'description' => 'Alcohol withdrawal stages, Delirium Tremens (DTs), Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome (thiamine deficiency triad), and pharmacological treatments.',
        'overview' => "Alcohol-Related Disorders encompass Alcohol Use Disorder, Alcohol Intoxication, Alcohol Withdrawal, and alcohol-induced neurocognitive and medical syndromes. Alcohol is a central nervous system depressant that exerts complex pharmacological actions: it functions as a positive allosteric modulator of inhibitory GABA-A receptors (enhancing inhibitory tone) and an antagonist of excitatory NMDA glutamate receptors (suppressing cognitive and motor excitation).\n\nWith chronic consumption, the brain undergoes profound compensatory neuroadaptation: GABA-A receptors downregulate, while NMDA glutamate receptors and voltage-gated calcium channels upregulate to restore homeostasis. When alcohol is abruptly discontinued, this compensatory equilibrium collapses, unmasking severe, life-threatening autonomic and glutamatergic hyperarousal.\n\nAlcohol withdrawal is a medical emergency that follows a predictable chronological progression: minor autonomic hyperactivity (tremors, sweating, nausea) within 6-12 hours; alcohol hallucinosis (visual/auditory hallucinations with clear sensorium) within 12-24 hours; generalized tonic-clonic withdrawal seizures ('rum fits') within 24-48 hours; and Delirium Tremens (DTs)—characterized by severe autonomic instability, hyperthermia, disorientation, and fluctuating delirium—within 48-96 hours. Furthermore, chronic nutritional malnutrition leads to Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome from thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Evaluating Alcohol Syndromes under DSM-5:\n\n1. Alcohol Intoxication: Clinically significant problematic behavioral changes (inappropriate aggression, mood lability, impaired judgment) developing during or shortly after alcohol ingestion, accompanied by at least one of: slurred speech, incoordination, unsteady gait, nystagmus, attention/memory impairment, stupor or coma.\n\n2. Alcohol Withdrawal: Cessation of (or reduction in) alcohol use that has been heavy and prolonged, with at least two of the following developing within several hours to a few days:\n• Autonomic hyperactivity (sweating, tachycardia > 100 bpm).\n• Increased hand tremor.\n• Insomnia.\n• Nausea or vomiting.\n• Transient visual, tactile, or auditory hallucinations or illusions.\n• Psychomotor agitation.\n• Anxiety.\n• Generalized tonic-clonic seizures.\n\n3. Wernicke's Encephalopathy Triad (Reversible if treated immediately with IV Thiamine):\n• Acute Encephalopathy (confusion, delirium).\n• Oculomotor Dysfunction (nystagmus, bilateral abducens [CN VI] palsy, conjugate gaze palsy).\n• Gait Ataxia (broad-based, cerebellar ataxia).\n(Progression to Korsakoff Syndrome: Irreversible anterograde and retrograde amnesia with prominent confabulation due to atrophy of the mammillary bodies).",
        'specifiers' => "Alcohol Withdrawal is specified: 'With perceptual disturbances' (if hallucinations occur with intact reality testing). Severity of withdrawal is quantified using the Clinical Institute Withdrawal Assessment for Alcohol (CIWA-Ar) scale.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Delirium Tremens (DTs) vs. Alcoholic Hallucinosis: In Alcoholic Hallucinosis, visual/auditory hallucinations occur with a CLEAR SENSORIUM (patient is oriented and alert); in Delirium Tremens, hallucinations occur in the context of DELIRIUM (disorientation, fluctuating consciousness, profound autonomic instability, fever).\n\nAlcohol Withdrawal vs. Benzodiazepine Withdrawal: Clinically indistinguishable, as both act on GABA-A receptors; benzodiazepine withdrawal carries an identical seizure and delirium risk.\n\nWernicke's Encephalopathy vs. Acute Stroke: Bilateral gaze palsy and acute confusion require emergent IV thiamine administration BEFORE glucose administration to prevent irreversible encephalopathy.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 52-year-old chronic heavy drinker admitted to the hospital for orthopedic surgery. 72 hours post-operatively, the patient becomes severely disoriented, combative, drenching the bed in sweat with a heart rate of 142 bpm, blood pressure 185/110 mmHg, temperature 38.8 C (101.8 F), and gross body tremors.\n\nThe patient screams that giant insects are crawling on the walls (visual hallucinations) and does not know where they are. Neurological exam reveals disorientation to time and place with fluctuating alertness. Meeting criteria for severe autonomic hyperarousal, fever, and delirium occurring 72 hours post-cessation, the diagnosis is Alcohol Withdrawal Delirium (Delirium Tremens), requiring immediate ICU transfer and aggressive intravenous benzodiazepine protocol.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: Alcohol withdrawal timeline: Tremors (6-12h), Hallucinosis (12-24h), Seizures (24-48h), Delirium Tremens (48-96h). Alcoholic hallucinosis has a clear sensorium; Delirium Tremens involves delirium, disorientation, fever, and severe autonomic instability with up to 5% mortality. Wernicke encephalopathy triad: Confusion, Ophthalmoplegia (nystagmus/CN VI palsy), and Ataxia; caused by Thiamine (B1) deficiency. ALWAYS give Thiamine BEFORE glucose to prevent precipitating acute Wernicke encephalopathy. Disulfiram, Naltrexone, and Acamprosate are FDA-approved for AUD.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Alcohol Withdrawal Chronological Ladder', 'icon' => '🪜', 'definition' => 'Autonomic tremor (6-12h) -> Hallucinosis (12-24h) -> Seizures (24-48h) -> Delirium Tremens (48-96h).'],
            ['title' => 'Sensorium Distinction in Hallucinations', 'icon' => '👁️', 'definition' => 'Alcoholic hallucinosis maintains clear orientation; Delirium Tremens features global disorientation and clouding.'],
            ['title' => 'The Wernicke Thiamine Triad', 'icon' => '🧠', 'definition' => 'Confusion, Ophthalmoplegia, and Ataxia; give IV thiamine prior to glucose to prevent mammillary body necrosis.'],
            ['title' => 'FDA-Approved Pharmacotherapies', 'icon' => '💊', 'definition' => 'Naltrexone (opioid antagonist reducing craving), Acamprosate (NMDA/GABA modulator), and Disulfiram (aldehyde dehydrogenase inhibitor).']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Delirium Tremens (DTs)', 'definition' => 'A severe, potentially fatal manifestation of alcohol withdrawal occurring 48-96 hours post-cessation, characterized by delirium, autonomic instability, and tremors.'],
            ['term' => 'Wernicke Encephalopathy', 'definition' => 'An acute, reversible neuropsychiatric emergency caused by thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency, presenting with confusion, ataxia, and ophthalmoplegia.'],
            ['term' => 'Korsakoff Syndrome', 'definition' => 'A chronic, irreversible amnestic disorder resulting from untreated Wernicke encephalopathy, characterized by severe anterograde amnesia and confabulation.'],
            ['term' => 'Confabulation', 'definition' => 'The fabrication of false biographical memories without conscious intent to deceive, classically observed in Korsakoff syndrome to fill memory voids.']
        ]
