    4 => [
        'title' => 'Opioid-Related Disorders (Use Disorder, Intoxication, Overdose Triad, Withdrawal)',
        'slug' => 'opioid-related-disorders-overdose-triad-withdrawal',
        'description' => 'Mu-opioid receptor pharmacology, the classic overdose triad, Naloxone rescue, withdrawal stigmata (piloerection), and MAT medications.',
        'overview' => "Opioid-Related Disorders represent a global public health crisis driven by high-potency synthetic opioids (fentanyl), prescription opioid analgesics, and heroin. Opioids exert their potent analgesic, euphoric, and sedating effects primarily through agonism of G-protein-coupled mu-opioid receptors (MOR) located throughout the periaqueductal gray, locus coeruleus, ventral tegmental area, and the brainstem respiratory rhythm generators in the medulla oblongata.\n\nAcute opioid toxicity carries high mortality through central respiratory depression: stimulation of mu receptors in the pre-Bötzinger complex of the medulla suppresses the brainstem's ventilatory responsiveness to elevated arterial carbon dioxide (hypercapnia) and hypoxia. The hallmark clinical emergency of acute opioid toxicity is the 'Opioid Overdose Triad': (1) Miosis (pinpoint pupils), (2) Severe Respiratory Depression (respiratory rate < 8-10 breaths per minute), and (3) Central Nervous System Depression (stupor or coma).\n\nIn contrast, while opioid withdrawal is excruciatingly painful, it is not inherently fatal in healthy adults (unlike alcohol or benzodiazepine withdrawal). Opioid withdrawal represents an acute rebound adrenergic storm: chronic opioid suppression of the locus coeruleus is abruptly unmasked, resulting in massive noradrenergic firing that drives severe agitation, vomiting, diarrhea, rhinorrhea, lacrimation, and piloerection ('cold turkey').",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Evaluating Opioid Syndromes under DSM-5:\n\n1. Opioid Intoxication: Clinically significant problematic behavioral changes (initial euphoria followed by apathy, dysphoria, psychomotor agitation/retardation, impaired judgment), pupillary constriction (miosis; or pupillary dilation due to anoxia in severe overdose), and at least one of: drowsiness or coma, slurred speech, impairment in attention or memory.\n\n2. The Opioid Overdose Triad (Medical Emergency):\n• Pinpoint Pupils (Miosis) - Exception: Meperidine and severe cerebral anoxia produce mydriasis.\n• Respiratory Depression (bradypnea, apnea, cyanosis).\n• Altered Mental Status (stupor, unresponsiveness, coma).\n(Immediate treatment: Intravenous, intramuscular, or intranasal Naloxone [Narcan]).\n\n3. Opioid Withdrawal: Cessation or reduction of heavy, prolonged opioid use (or administration of an opioid antagonist), with at least three of the following nine features developing within minutes to several days:\n• Dysphoric mood.\n• Nausea or vomiting.\n• Muscle aches (myalgias).\n• Lacrimation (tearing) or rhinorrhea (runny nose).\n• Pupillary dilation (mydriasis), piloerection (goosebumps), or sweating.\n• Diarrhea.\n• Yawning (highly specific early sign).\n• Fever.\n• Insomnia.",
        'specifiers' => "Specifiers for Opioid Use Disorder: 'On maintenance therapy' (methadone or buprenorphine) or 'In a controlled environment'.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Opioid Overdose vs. Sedative-Hypnotic Overdose: Both cause coma and respiratory depression; however, pinpoint pupils (miosis) are characteristic of opioids, while benzodiazepines typically present with normal or sluggish pupils and respond to flumazenil.\n\nOpioid Withdrawal vs. Influenza / Viral Gastroenteritis: Opioid withdrawal shares nausea, diarrhea, and fever, but is distinguished by pupillary dilation (mydriasis), frequent yawning, piloerection, and rhinorrhea.\n\nClonidine for Withdrawal: Centrally acting alpha-2 adrenergic agonists (clonidine, lofexidine) reduce autonomic noradrenergic outflow from the locus coeruleus, relieving withdrawal symptoms.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 24-year-old found unconscious in a restroom with blue lips, a respiratory rate of 4 breaths per minute, heart rate 48 bpm, pinpoint pupils (1 mm bilateral), and needle track marks on arms.\n\nEmergency medical technicians administer 4 mg intranasal Naloxone. Within 90 seconds, the patient regains consciousness, breathes at 18 breaths per minute, and immediately exhibits severe yawning, shivering, rhinorrhea, dilated pupils (7 mm), and profuse vomiting (precipitated opioid withdrawal). The prompt reversal of the overdose triad confirms acute Opioid Toxicity.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: The Opioid Overdose Triad is Miosis (pinpoint pupils), Respiratory Depression, and Coma; treat immediately with Naloxone (Narcan). Exception: Meperidine (Demerol) intoxication causes dilated pupils (mydriasis) due to anticholinergic properties. Opioid withdrawal features: yawning (classic early sign), lacrimation, rhinorrhea, piloerection ('cold turkey'), mydriasis, and diarrhea; withdrawal is miserable but rarely fatal. Medication-Assisted Treatment (MAT) gold standards: Methadone (full mu agonist), Buprenorphine (partial mu agonist), Naltrexone (antagonist).",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'The Classic Overdose Triad', 'icon' => '🚨', 'definition' => 'Miosis (pinpoint pupils), Respiratory Depression (<8-10 bpm), and Coma/Stupor; immediate indication for naloxone.'],
            ['title' => 'Pre-Bötzinger Respiratory Suppression', 'icon' => '🫁', 'definition' => 'Mu-opioid receptor activation in brainstem respiratory centers blunts responsiveness to hypercapnia, driving fatal apnea.'],
            ['title' => 'Locus Coeruleus Noradrenergic Rebound', 'icon' => '⚡', 'definition' => 'Opioid withdrawal unmasks hyperactive locus coeruleus noradrenaline release, driving autonomic withdrawal signs.'],
            ['title' => 'Medication-Assisted Treatment (MAT)', 'icon' => '💊', 'definition' => 'Methadone (full agonist), Buprenorphine (partial agonist with ceiling effect), and Naltrexone (antagonist) cut mortality by 50%+.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Naloxone (Narcan)', 'definition' => 'A pure competitive mu-opioid receptor antagonist that rapidly displaces opioids, completely reversing acute respiratory arrest within minutes.'],
            ['term' => 'Piloerection', 'definition' => 'Involuntary erection of hair follicles (goosebumps) occurring during opioid withdrawal, origin of the phrase \'cold turkey\'.'],
            ['term' => 'Buprenorphine', 'definition' => 'A high-affinity, partial mu-opioid receptor agonist that suppresses cravings and withdrawal while possessing a ceiling effect on respiratory depression.'],
            ['term' => 'Meperidine (Demerol)', 'definition' => 'A synthetic opioid with anticholinergic properties; toxicity produces mydriasis (dilated pupils) and seizures due to its active metabolite normeperidine.']
        ]
