    5 => [
        'title' => 'Stimulant-Related Disorders (Cocaine, Amphetamines) & Cannabis Disorders',
        'slug' => 'stimulants-cocaine-amphetamines-cannabis-disorders',
        'description' => 'Sympathomimetic toxicity, cocaine vs. amphetamine pharmacology, stimulant psychosis, and Cannabis Hyperemesis Syndrome (hot shower sign).',
        'overview' => "Stimulant-Related and Cannabis-Related Disorders encompass clinical conditions arising from the use of central nervous system sympathomimetic agents (cocaine, prescription amphetamines, methamphetamine) and cannabinoids. Stimulants and cannabis represent two of the most widely used illicit and recreational substance classes worldwide, carrying distinct pharmacological mechanisms, psychiatric toxicities, and withdrawal syndromes.\n\nCocaine acts primarily by blocking the presynaptic reuptake transporters for dopamine (DAT), norepinephrine (NET), and serotonin (SERT), producing acute extracellular neurotransmitter accumulation. In contrast, amphetamines and methamphetamine exert a dual mechanism: they not only block reuptake transporters, but also reverse transporter flow and inhibit vesicular monoamine transporter 2 (VMAT-2), forcibly releasing massive floods of cytoplasmic dopamine into the synapse. Stimulant intoxication produces a hyperadrenergic state (tachycardia, hypertension, mydriasis, diaphoresis) and acute psychiatric complications, including paranoid stimulant psychosis and tactile formication ('crank bugs').\n\nCannabis exerts psychoactive effects through Delta-9-tetrahydrocannabinol (THC) agonism at cannabinoid CB1 receptors in the basal ganglia, hippocampus, and cortex. A major emerging clinical condition in DSM-5 is Cannabis Hyperemesis Syndrome—a paradoxical cyclical vomiting condition occurring in chronic heavy cannabis users characterized by severe nausea, intractable vomiting, and abdominal pain that is uniquely relieved by compulsive hot showering or bathing.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Evaluating Stimulant and Cannabis Syndromes under DSM-5:\n\n1. Stimulant Intoxication: Problematic behavioral changes (euphoria, hypervigilance, interpersonal sensitivity, anxiety, anger, impaired judgment) and two (or more) of the following:\n• Tachycardia or bradycardia.\n• Pupillary dilation (mydriasis).\n• Elevated or lowered blood pressure.\n• Perspiration or chills.\n• Nausea or vomiting.\n• Weight loss.\n• Psychomotor agitation or retardation.\n• Muscular weakness, respiratory depression, chest pain, or cardiac arrhythmias.\n• Confusion, seizures, dyskinesias, dystonias, or coma.\n\n2. Stimulant Withdrawal ('The Crash'): Dysphoric mood and two (or more) of: fatigue, vivid unpleasant dreams, insomnia or hypersomnia, increased appetite (hyperphagia), psychomotor retardation or agitation.\n\n3. Cannabis Withdrawal: Cessation of heavy, prolonged cannabis use, with at least three developing within 1 week: irritability/anger/aggression, nervousness/anxiety, sleep difficulty (insomnia/disturbing dreams), decreased appetite or weight loss, restlessness, depressed mood, and at least one physical symptom (abdominal pain, shakiness/tremors, sweating, fever, chills, headache).\n\n4. Cannabis Hyperemesis Syndrome: Recurrent severe nausea, vomiting, and colicky abdominal pain in chronic daily users, displaying pathognomonic relief from hot water immersion.",
        'specifiers' => "Stimulant Intoxication is specified: 'With perceptual disturbances' (if hallucinations occur with intact reality testing). Course specifiers include 'In early remission' and 'In sustained remission'.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Stimulant Intoxication vs. Opioid Intoxication: Stimulants produce MYDRIASIS (dilated pupils), tachycardia, and hypertension; opioids produce MIOSIS (pinpoint pupils), bradycardia, and bradypnea.\n\nStimulant Psychosis vs. Primary Schizophrenia: Stimulant psychosis features prominent persecutory delusions and visual/tactile hallucinations (formication bugs) with tachycardia and dilated pupils; resolves within days to weeks of verified abstinence.\n\nCannabis Hyperemesis vs. Cyclic Vomiting Syndrome: Cannabis hyperemesis is confirmed when cyclical vomiting resolves completely following sustained cannabis cessation.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 26-year-old brought to the emergency department by police with extreme paranoia. The patient is sweating profusely, has bilateral dilated pupils (mydriasis, 8 mm), blood pressure 180/110 mmHg, and heart rate 136 bpm.\n\nThe patient is frantically scratching their forearms, screaming that microscopic parasites are burrowing beneath their skin (tactile formication), and asserting that federal agents are tracking them through satellites. Urine drug screen is strongly positive for methamphetamine. Meeting criteria for hyperadrenergic arousal, dilated pupils, persecutory delusions, and formication, the clinical formulation confirms Stimulant Intoxication, With perceptual disturbances (Methamphetamine).",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: Stimulants (cocaine, amphetamines) produce MYDRIASIS (dilated pupils), tachycardia, hypertension, and paranoia. Formication (tactile sensation of bugs under skin) is classic for cocaine/methamphetamine toxicity. Stimulant withdrawal ('the crash') features hypersomnia, hyperphagia, severe fatigue, and vivid dreams. Cannabis withdrawal was newly added to DSM-5 (irritability, insomnia, anorexia, restlessness). Cannabis Hyperemesis Syndrome is characterized by cyclical vomiting uniquely relieved by hot showers.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Sympathomimetic Toxic Triad', 'icon' => '⚡', 'definition' => 'Mydriasis (dilated pupils), tachycardia, and psychomotor agitation define acute stimulant intoxication.'],
            ['title' => 'Tactile Formication Hallucination', 'icon' => '🐜', 'definition' => 'The sensation of insects crawling under the skin (\'crank bugs\') driving severe excoriation in chronic methamphetamine toxicity.'],
            ['title' => 'The Stimulant Crash Withdrawal', 'icon' => '😴', 'definition' => 'Marked by hypersomnia, ravenous appetite (hyperphagia), severe exhaustion, and profound depressive dysphoria.'],
            ['title' => 'Hot Shower Sign in Cannabis Hyperemesis', 'icon' => '🚿', 'definition' => 'Compulsive hot bathing temporarily resets altered hypothalamic thermoregulation in chronic heavy cannabis users.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Mydriasis', 'definition' => 'Pupillary dilation, a classic physical sign of sympathomimetic stimulant intoxication (cocaine, amphetamines) and opioid withdrawal.'],
            ['term' => 'Formication', 'definition' => 'A specific tactile hallucination involving the perception of insects crawling on or beneath the skin, associated with cocaine and methamphetamine.'],
            ['term' => 'Cannabis Hyperemesis Syndrome (CHS)', 'definition' => 'A clinical syndrome of cyclical intractable nausea, vomiting, and abdominal pain in chronic daily cannabis users, relieved by hot showers.'],
            ['term' => 'VMAT-2 Inhibition', 'definition' => 'The pharmacological mechanism whereby amphetamines disrupt vesicular storage, forcing massive dopamine release into the cytoplasm.']
        ]
    ],
    6 => [
        'title' => 'Gambling Disorder: The Sole Non-Substance Behavioral Addiction in DSM-5',
        'slug' => 'gambling-disorder-behavioral-addiction',
        'description' => 'The 4 of 9 criteria threshold within 12 months, chasing losses phenomenon, elimination of legal problems, and mesolimbic reward shared circuitry.',
        'overview' => "Gambling Disorder represents a historic taxonomic milestone in DSM-5: it is the sole non-substance-related, behavioral condition classified alongside chemical substance use disorders in the Substance-Related and Addictive Disorders chapter. In DSM-IV, pathological gambling was classified as an impulse-control disorder alongside kleptomania and pyromania. DSM-5 relocated gambling based on massive neuroimaging, genetic, and neurochemical evidence proving that gambling activates identical mesolimbic dopamine reward circuitry, produces clinical tolerance and withdrawal-like phenomena, and shares high genetic comorbidity with substance addictions.\n\nThe core psychopathology of Gambling Disorder is characterized by persistent and recurrent problematic gambling behavior leading to clinically significant impairment or distress, operationalized by meeting at least four of nine criteria within a 12-month period. Behaviors mirror chemical addictions: individuals need to gamble with increasing amounts of money to achieve desired excitement (tolerance), experience restlessness and irritability when attempting to cut down (withdrawal), and make repeated unsuccessful attempts to control gambling.\n\nA pathognomonic cognitive and behavioral marker of Gambling Disorder is 'chasing losses'—returning another day to win back lost money, driven by cognitive distortions such as the gambler's fallacy (believing past random events influence future probabilities). Furthermore, DSM-5 eliminated the historical criterion of 'committing illegal acts' (e.g., forgery, fraud, theft to finance gambling) to improve diagnostic consistency.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Criterion A: Persistent and recurrent problematic gambling behavior leading to clinically significant impairment or distress, as indicated by the individual exhibiting four (or more) of the following in a 12-month period:\n1. Needs to gamble with increasing amounts of money in order to achieve the desired excitement (Tolerance equivalent).\n2. Is restless or irritable when attempting to cut down or stop gambling (Withdrawal equivalent).\n3. Has made repeated unsuccessful efforts to control, cut back, or stop gambling.\n4. Is often preoccupied with gambling (e.g., persistent thoughts of reliving past gambling experiences, planning the next venture, thinking of ways to get money to gamble).\n5. Often gambles when feeling distressed (e.g., helpless, guilty, anxious, depressed).\n6. After losing money gambling, often returns another day to get even ('chasing' one's losses - PATHOGNOMONIC MARKER).\n7. Lies to conceal the extent of involvement with gambling.\n8. Has jeopardized or lost a significant relationship, job, or educational or career opportunity because of gambling.\n9. Relies on others to provide money to relieve desperate financial situations caused by gambling ('bailout').\n\nCriterion B: The gambling behavior is not better explained by a manic episode.\n\nSeverity Grading (within a 12-month period):\n• Mild: 4–5 criteria met.\n• Moderate: 6–7 criteria met.\n• Severe: 8–9 criteria met.",
        'specifiers' => "Course Specifiers: 'Episodic' (meeting diagnostic criteria at more than one time point, with symptoms subsiding between periods of gambling for at least several months) or 'Persistent' (experiencing continuous symptoms, meeting criteria over a period of multiple years).\n\nRemission Specifiers: 'In early remission' (none of the criteria met for at least 3 months but less than 12 months) or 'In sustained remission' (none of the criteria met for 12 months or longer).",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Gambling Disorder vs. Manic Episode: Gambling during a manic episode is driven by grandiosity, expansive mood, and decreased sleep need; if gambling occurs exclusively during mania, Bipolar Disorder is diagnosed (Criterion B exclusion).\n\nGambling Disorder vs. Social / Professional Gambling: Social gamblers gamble with friends for recreation and have predetermined loss limits; professional gamblers treat gambling as an occupation with disciplined risk management; neither exhibits loss of control or chases losses.\n\nDSM-IV vs. DSM-5 Differences: DSM-5 lowered the threshold from 5 to 4 criteria and eliminated the illegal acts criterion.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 44-year-old accountant who began sports betting two years ago. The accountant now bets $5,000 per game to achieve excitement (tolerance), becomes irritable when attempting to delete betting apps, constantly checks live odds at work, and gambles when depressed.\n\nLast month, after losing $30,000, the accountant returned to the casino the following three days betting larger sums to 'win it all back' (chasing losses). The accountant lied to their spouse, drained children's college savings, and asked parents for an emergency loan to prevent foreclosure (bailout). There has never been a manic episode. Meeting 7 of 9 criteria, the diagnosis is Gambling Disorder, Moderate, Persistent.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: Gambling Disorder is the ONLY non-substance behavioral addiction in DSM-5. Requires at least 4 of 9 criteria within a 12-month period. Key features: tolerance (increasing money for excitement), withdrawal (irritability when stopping), chasing losses (returning another day to get even; pathognomonic), lying, and bailouts. DSM-5 eliminated the illegal acts criterion. Must rule out Bipolar Mania (Criterion B). Cognitive distortions include the Gambler's Fallacy and Illusion of Control.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Sole Behavioral Addiction in DSM-5', 'icon' => '🎰', 'definition' => 'Relocated from impulse-control to addictive disorders based on shared mesolimbic dopamine neurobiology.'],
            ['title' => 'The 4 of 9 Annual Threshold', 'icon' => '🔢', 'definition' => 'Requires meeting at least 4 of 9 criteria within a 12-month period (reduced from 5 criteria in DSM-IV).'],
            ['title' => 'The Chasing Losses Hallmark', 'icon' => '💸', 'definition' => 'Returning another day to recoup lost money represents the definitive cognitive-behavioral marker of pathological gambling.'],
            ['title' => 'Exclusion During Bipolar Mania', 'icon' => '🚫', 'definition' => 'If excessive gambling occurs exclusively during an acute manic episode, Bipolar I Disorder takes precedence.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Chasing Losses', 'definition' => 'The persistent behavioral pattern of returning to gamble after losing money, driven by the irrational conviction that future bets will recoup losses.'],
            ['term' => 'Gambler\'s Fallacy', 'definition' => 'The erroneous cognitive belief that past independent random events (e.g., coin flips or roulette spins) alter the probability of future random outcomes.'],
            ['term' => 'Illusion of Control', 'definition' => 'A cognitive distortion wherein a gambler believes that personal skill, rituals, or knowledge can influence purely random games of chance.'],
            ['term' => 'Bailout', 'definition' => 'Relying on family, friends, or third parties to provide money to relieve desperate, catastrophic financial insolvency caused by gambling.']
        ]
    ],
    7 => [
        'title' => 'Oppositional Defiant Disorder (ODD: Angry/Irritable, Argumentative, Vindictiveness)',
        'slug' => 'oppositional-defiant-disorder-odd-criteria',
        'description' => 'The 3 symptom dimensions (Angry/Irritable, Argumentative/Defiant, Vindictiveness), 6-month persistence, non-sibling interaction, and DMDD exclusion.',
        'overview' => "Oppositional Defiant Disorder (ODD) is a disruptive behavior disorder characterized by a persistent pattern of angry/irritable mood, argumentative/defiant behavior, or vindictiveness lasting for at least six months. In DSM-5, ODD is categorized within the Disruptive, Impulse-Control, and Conduct Disorders chapter. Unlike normal developmental childhood stubbornness, ODD produces significant functional impairment across family, academic, or social environments.\n\nA fundamental structural advancement in DSM-5 was organizing ODD criteria into three distinct symptom dimensions: (1) Angry/Irritable Mood (frequent loss of temper, touchiness, anger); (2) Argumentative/Defiant Behavior (arguing with authority figures, active non-compliance, deliberately annoying others, blaming others for mistakes); and (3) Vindictiveness (spiteful or vindictive behavior at least twice in six months). These three dimensions exhibit distinct developmental trajectories: the angry/irritable dimension strongly predicts future depressive and anxiety disorders, whereas the argumentative and vindictive dimensions predict Conduct Disorder.\n\nFurthermore, DSM-5 instituted developmental frequency benchmarks to prevent over-diagnosing normal toddler and adolescent defiance. In children under 5 years, behavior must occur on most days for at least six months; in individuals 5 years or older, behavior must occur at least once per week for at least six months. Crucially, symptoms must be exhibited with at least one individual who is NOT a sibling.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Criterion A: A pattern of angry/irritable mood, argumentative/defiant behavior, or vindictiveness lasting at least 6 months as evidenced by at least four symptoms from any of the following categories, exhibited during interaction with at least one individual who is not a sibling:\n\nAngry/Irritable Mood:\n1. Often loses temper.\n2. Is often touchy or easily annoyed.\n3. Is often angry and resentful.\n\nArgumentative/Defiant Behavior:\n4. Often argues with authority figures or, for children and adolescents, with adults.\n5. Often actively defies or refuses to comply with requests from authority figures or with rules.\n6. Often deliberately annoys others.\n7. Often blames others for his or her mistakes or misbehavior.\n\nVindictiveness:\n8. Has been spiteful or vindictive at least twice within the past 6 months.\n\nDevelopmental Frequency Benchmarks:\n• Children under 5 years: Behavior should occur on most days for at least 6 months.\n• Individuals 5 years or older: Behavior should occur at least once per week for at least 6 months.\n\nCriterion B: Causes distress in the individual or others in their immediate social context, or impacts negatively on social, educational, or occupational functioning.\n\nCriterion C: Behaviors do not occur exclusively during psychotic, substance, depressive, or bipolar disorders, and criteria are NOT met for Disruptive Mood Dysregulation Disorder (DMDD).",
        'specifiers' => "Severity Grading across settings:\n• Mild: Symptoms are confined to only one setting (e.g., at home only, at school only, or with peers only).\n• Moderate: Some symptoms are present in at least two settings.\n• Severe: Some symptoms are present in three or more settings.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "ODD vs. Disruptive Mood Dysregulation Disorder (DMDD): Absolute hierarchy rule: If criteria for both ODD and DMDD are met, ONLY DMDD is diagnosed. DMDD features severe rages >= 3x/week and chronic baseline irritability.\n\nODD vs. Conduct Disorder (CD): Conduct Disorder involves severe violations of the basic rights of others or societal norms (cruelty to animals, physical fighting with weapons, stealing, property destruction); ODD does NOT involve severe physical aggression or destruction of property.\n\nODD vs. Normal Sibling Rivalry: Symptoms must be demonstrated with at least one NON-SIBLING (teachers, parents, peers).",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves an 8-year-old child whose teachers and parents report chronic friction for eight months. The child argues daily with adults, refuses to line up or complete assignments, deliberately knocks over classmates' pencils to irritate them, blames peers for classroom disruptions, and is easily annoyed.\n\nThe child has never damaged property, never stolen, never been physically cruel to animals, and does not have explosive physical rages. Meeting 5 criteria across argumentative and irritable dimensions for >6 months with non-siblings in both home and school settings, the clinical formulation confirms Oppositional Defiant Disorder, Moderate Severity.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: ODD requires at least 4 symptoms across 3 dimensions (Angry/Irritable, Argumentative/Defiant, Vindictive) lasting >= 6 months with at least one NON-SIBLING. Frequency rule: >= once per week for age 5+; most days for under age 5. Vindictiveness requires at least twice in 6 months. Absolute hierarchy: DMDD overrides ODD (if both criteria met, code DMDD only). ODD lacks the physical cruelty, destruction, and serious theft seen in Conduct Disorder. Parent Management Training (PMT) is first-line.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Three Distinct Symptom Clusters', 'icon' => '🔺', 'definition' => 'Angry/Irritable (predicts depression), Argumentative/Defiant, and Vindictive (predicts conduct disorder).'],
            ['title' => 'The Non-Sibling Interaction Rule', 'icon' => '👥', 'definition' => 'Behaviors must be observed with at least one non-sibling individual to rule out ordinary sibling rivalry.'],
            ['title' => 'The DMDD Diagnostic Override', 'icon' => '👑', 'definition' => 'If a child satisfies criteria for both ODD and DMDD, DMDD takes absolute precedence and ODD is not coded.'],
            ['title' => 'Parent Management Training (PMT)', 'icon' => '🛠️', 'definition' => 'The evidence-based behavioral intervention of choice, training parents in operant reinforcement and consistent discipline.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Parent Management Training (PMT)', 'definition' => 'A structured behavioral intervention that trains parents in positive reinforcement, clear commands, and consistent non-violent discipline for ODD.'],
            ['term' => 'Vindictiveness', 'definition' => 'Spiteful, vengeful behavior executed to harm or retaliate against another person, requiring at least two occurrences in six months in ODD.'],
            ['term' => 'Non-Compliance', 'definition' => 'Active, deliberate refusal to obey direct instructions or follow established rules set by authority figures.'],
            ['term' => 'Coercive Family Process', 'definition' => 'A behavioral cycle described by Gerald Patterson wherein child misbehavior escalates until parents surrender, reinforcing defiance.']
        ]
    ],
    8 => [
        'title' => 'Conduct Disorder (CD: Aggression, Property Destruction, Deceit/Theft, Violations)',
        'slug' => 'conduct-disorder-criteria-prosocial-specifier',
        'description' => 'The 4 behavioral domains (15 criteria; 3 of 15 threshold), childhood vs. adolescent onset, and the limited prosocial emotions specifier.',
        'overview' => "Conduct Disorder (CD) is a severe psychiatric disorder characterized by a repetitive and persistent pattern of behavior in which the basic rights of others or major age-appropriate societal norms or rules are violated. In DSM-5, Conduct Disorder represents the childhood and adolescent precursor to adult Antisocial Personality Disorder (ASPD); indeed, a documented history of Conduct Disorder with onset before age 15 is an absolute mandatory requirement for diagnosing Antisocial Personality Disorder in adulthood.\n\nConduct Disorder behaviors are categorized into four major domains: (1) Aggression to people and animals; (2) Destruction of property; (3) Deceitfulness or theft; and (4) Serious violations of rules. To satisfy diagnostic criteria, an individual must exhibit at least three of fifteen specific behavioral criteria within the preceding 12 months, with at least one criterion present in the past six months.\n\nA landmark innovation in DSM-5 was the introduction of the 'With limited prosocial emotions' specifier. This specifier operationalizes callous-unemotional traits (lack of remorse, lack of empathy, unconcern about performance, shallow affect), identifying a biologically and developmentally distinct subgroup of children who exhibit severe, proactive aggression, diminished autonomic fear reactivity, and poor response to standard behavioral interventions.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Criterion A: A repetitive and persistent pattern of behavior in which the basic rights of others or major age-appropriate societal norms or rules are violated, manifested by the presence of at least three of the following 15 criteria in the past 12 months, with at least one criterion present in the past 6 months:\n\nAggression to People and Animals:\n1. Often bullies, threatens, or intimidates others.\n2. Often initiates physical fights.\n3. Has used a weapon that can cause serious physical harm (e.g., bat, brick, broken bottle, knife, gun).\n4. Has been physically cruel to people.\n5. Has been physically cruel to animals.\n6. Has stolen while confronting a victim (e.g., mugging, purse snatching, armed robbery).\n7. Has forced someone into sexual activity.\n\nDestruction of Property:\n8. Has deliberately engaged in fire setting with the intention of causing serious damage.\n9. Has deliberately destroyed others' property (other than by fire setting).\n\nDeceitfulness or Theft:\n10. Has broken into someone else's house, building, or car.\n11. Often lies to obtain goods or favors or to avoid obligations (i.e., 'cons' others).\n12. Has stolen items of nontrivial value without confronting a victim (e.g., shoplifting, forgery).\n\nSerious Violations of Rules:\n13. Often stays out at night despite parental prohibitions, beginning before age 13 years.\n14. Has run away from home overnight at least twice while living in parental home, or once without returning for a lengthy period.\n15. Is often truant from school, beginning before age 13 years.\n\nCriterion B: Causes clinically significant impairment in social, academic, or occupational functioning.\nCriterion C: If 18 years or older, criteria are NOT met for Antisocial Personality Disorder.",
        'specifiers' => "Age-at-Onset Subtypes:\n• Childhood-onset type: Individuals show at least one symptom characteristic of conduct disorder prior to age 10 years (male predominance, high aggression, strongly predicts adult Antisocial Personality Disorder).\n• Adolescent-onset type: Individuals show no symptoms characteristic of conduct disorder prior to age 10 years (more equal gender ratio, better prognosis, rebellious peer group behavior).\n• Unspecified onset: Age at onset is not known.\n\nWith Limited Prosocial Emotions Specifier (Callous-Unemotional Traits):\nRequires meeting at least two of the following four characteristics persistently over at least 12 months across multiple relationships and settings:\n1. Lack of remorse or guilt (does not feel bad or guilty when doing something wrong, except when caught).\n2. Callous - lack of empathy (disregards and is unconcerned about feelings of others; cold and uncaring).\n3. Unconcerned about performance (does not show concern about poor/problematic performance at school or work).\n4. Shallow or deficient affect (does not express feelings or show emotions, except in shallow, insincere ways).",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Conduct Disorder vs. Oppositional Defiant Disorder: ODD involves verbal defiance, irritability, and arguing, but LACKS severe physical aggression, cruelty to animals, property destruction, and theft; if criteria for CD are met, Conduct Disorder takes precedence.\n\nConduct Disorder vs. Antisocial Personality Disorder: In individuals aged 18 and older, if criteria for ASPD are met, Antisocial Personality Disorder is diagnosed. (History of CD before age 15 is required for ASPD).\n\nConduct Disorder vs. ADHD: ADHD causes impulsive rule-breaking due to inattention and hyperactivity, without deliberate intent to violate rights or harm animals.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 14-year-old male evaluated in juvenile court. Over the past year, he has initiated six physical fights with weapons, tortured neighborhood cats (cruelty to animals), and broken into two neighborhood garages stealing electronics (3 criteria across aggression and theft).\n\nHe expresses zero guilt, mocking the victims and stating: 'They deserved it for being weak.' Psychological testing and multi-informant reports confirm a pervasive lack of empathy, callousness, and lack of remorse across all settings for over a year. Meeting criteria for aggression and theft with callousness, the clinical formulation confirms Conduct Disorder, Childhood-onset type, With limited prosocial emotions.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: Conduct Disorder requires at least 3 of 15 criteria across 4 domains (Aggression, Property destruction, Deceitfulness/theft, Rule violations) in 12 months. Childhood-onset type (onset before age 10) strongly predicts adult Antisocial Personality Disorder. The 'With limited prosocial emotions' specifier identifies callous-unemotional traits (lack of remorse, lack of empathy, unconcerned about performance, shallow affect). A history of Conduct Disorder before age 15 is MANDATORY for diagnosing adult Antisocial Personality Disorder.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Four Core Behavioral Domains', 'icon' => '🏛️', 'definition' => 'Aggression to people/animals, Destruction of property, Deceitfulness/theft, and Serious rule violations (15 criteria).'],
            ['title' => 'The 3 of 15 Annual Threshold', 'icon' => '🔢', 'definition' => 'Requires at least 3 symptoms in the past 12 months, with at least 1 symptom present in the past 6 months.'],
            ['title' => 'Childhood vs. Adolescent Onset', 'icon' => '🌱', 'definition' => 'Childhood onset (before age 10) carries high violence and predicts adult ASPD; adolescent onset has better prognosis.'],
            ['title' => 'Limited Prosocial Emotions Specifier', 'icon' => '🧊', 'definition' => 'Callous-unemotional traits (lack of remorse, callousness, unconcerned about performance, shallow affect) driving proactive aggression.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Callous-Unemotional (CU) Traits', 'definition' => 'A distinct temperamental pattern characterized by diminished empathy, lack of guilt, and uncaring attitudes, operationalized under Limited Prosocial Emotions.'],
            ['term' => 'Proactive Aggression', 'definition' => 'Deliberate, cold-blooded, goal-directed aggressive behavior used to obtain power, money, or peer dominance, common in CU traits.'],
            ['term' => 'Reactive Aggression', 'definition' => 'Hot-tempered, impulsive aggressive behavior triggered by perceived provocation, threat, or frustration.'],
            ['term' => 'Antisocial Personality Precursor', 'definition' => 'The mandatory diagnostic rule that an individual must have exhibited Conduct Disorder before age 15 to be diagnosed with ASPD at age 18.']
        ]
    ],
    9 => [
        'title' => 'Pyromania & Kleptomania: Pathological Tension, Pleasure, and Relief Cycles',
        'slug' => 'pyromania-kleptomania-tension-relief-cycles',
        'description' => 'Impulse control disorders, deliberate fire-setting and stealing of unneeded objects, tension-pleasure-relief cycles, and forensic rule-outs.',
        'overview' => "Pyromania and Kleptomania represent pure impulse-control disorders classified within the Disruptive, Impulse-Control, and Conduct Disorders chapter of DSM-5. Unlike conduct disorder or criminal behavior—which are driven by monetary gain, anger, vengeance, political terrorism, or gang status—pyromania and kleptomania are defined by a distinct neurobiological cycle of internal psychological tension, compulsive behavioral enactment, and subsequent emotional relief.\n\nIn Pyromania, the core pathology is deliberate and purposeful fire setting on more than one occasion, preceded by tension or affective arousal, and followed by pleasure, gratification, or relief when setting fires or witnessing their aftermath. Crucially, the fire setting cannot be motivated by monetary gain (arson for insurance fraud), political ideology, anger, vengeance, or impaired judgment in psychosis or dementia.\n\nIn Kleptomania, the defining feature is the recurrent failure to resist impulses to steal objects that are NOT needed for personal use or for their monetary value. The individual experiences rising subjective tension immediately before the theft, followed by pleasure, gratification, or relief at the time of committing the theft. Stolen items are typically hoarded, given away, secretly returned, or discarded, distinguishing kleptomania from ordinary shoplifting.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Diagnostic Criteria for Specific Impulse-Control Disorders:\n\n1. Pyromania:\nCriterion A: Deliberate and purposeful fire setting on more than one occasion.\nCriterion B: Tension or affective arousal before the act.\nCriterion C: Fascination with, interest in, curiosity about, or attraction to fire and its situational contexts (e.g., paraphernalia, uses, consequences).\nCriterion D: Pleasure, gratification, or relief when setting fires or when witnessing or participating in their aftermath.\nCriterion E: The fire setting is NOT done for monetary gain, as an expression of sociopolitical ideology, to conceal criminal activity, to express anger or vengeance, to improve one's living circumstances, in response to a delusion/hallucination, or as a result of impaired judgment.\nCriterion F: Not better explained by Conduct Disorder, a manic episode, or Antisocial Personality Disorder.\n\n2. Kleptomania:\nCriterion A: Recurrent failure to resist impulses to steal objects that are not needed for personal use or for their monetary value.\nCriterion B: Increasing sense of tension immediately before committing the theft.\nCriterion C: Pleasure, gratification, or relief at the time of committing the theft.\nCriterion D: The stealing is NOT committed to express anger or vengeance and is not in response to a delusion or hallucination.\nCriterion E: The stealing is not better explained by Conduct Disorder, a manic episode, or Antisocial Personality Disorder.",
        'specifiers' => "Both disorders are coded independently, documenting clinical course, legal involvement, and comorbid mood, anxiety, or substance use conditions.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Kleptomania vs. Shoplifting (Malingering/Theft): Shoplifting is planned and motivated by the usefulness or monetary value of the stolen item; in Kleptomania, items have zero personal use or value and thefts are impulsive.\n\nPyromania vs. Arson: Arson is a legal crime committed for financial profit (insurance fraud), revenge, concealment of murder, or sabotage; Pyromania is a rare psychiatric illness driven strictly by internal tension and fascination with fire.\n\nKleptomania vs. Bipolar Mania: Stealing during mania is part of generalized grandiosity and disinhibition; Kleptomania features isolated tension-relief cycles during euthymia.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 36-year-old wealthy executive caught shoplifting plastic keychains and baby bibs from a department store. The executive has $50,000 in personal savings and has no children.\n\nThe executive reports that while shopping, an overwhelming, uncomfortable wave of tension surged through their body, which vanished and was replaced by intense pleasure and relief the moment the keychains were slipped into a pocket. The executive had 40 identical stolen keychains hidden in a shoebox at home. The theft of items not needed for use or monetary value driven by tension-relief confirms Kleptomania.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: Both Pyromania and Kleptomania follow a 3-part cycle: (1) Tension before the act, (2) Pleasure/relief during/after the act, (3) ABSENCE of external motivation (money, revenge, anger, politics). Kleptomania involves stealing items NOT needed for personal use or monetary value (distinguishing it from shoplifting). Pyromania involves deliberate fire setting for internal gratification (distinguishing it from arson). Both cannot be diagnosed if occurring exclusively during a Manic episode, Conduct Disorder, or ASPD.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Tension-Pleasure-Relief Cycle', 'icon' => '🔄', 'definition' => 'Internal psychological tension builds before the act, resolving into acute pleasure and relief during execution.'],
            ['title' => 'Absence of External Gain Rule', 'icon' => '🚫', 'definition' => 'Acts are not motivated by money, revenge, political ideology, or concealing crime (ruling out arson and shoplifting).'],
            ['title' => 'Kleptomanic Object Worthlessness', 'icon' => '🔑', 'definition' => 'Stolen items have no personal utility or financial value to the patient, frequently being hoarded or given away.'],
            ['title' => 'Exclusion During Mania or CD', 'icon' => '⚖️', 'definition' => 'Cannot be diagnosed if behaviors occur exclusively during acute Bipolar Mania, Conduct Disorder, or ASPD.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Kleptomania', 'definition' => 'An impulse-control disorder characterized by recurrent failure to resist urges to steal unneeded, non-monetary items, preceded by tension and followed by relief.'],
            ['term' => 'Pyromania', 'definition' => 'An impulse-control disorder characterized by deliberate, repeated fire setting driven by fascination with fire, preceded by tension and followed by gratification.'],
            ['term' => 'Arson', 'definition' => 'The criminal act of deliberately setting fire to property, motivated by financial gain, revenge, political protest, or criminal concealment.'],
            ['term' => 'Ego-Dystonic Impulsivity', 'definition' => 'A clinical paradox where an individual experiences immense shame and guilt regarding their impulsive thefts while feeling powerless against the urge.']
        ]
    ],
    10 => [
        'title' => 'Chapter Mastery & Comprehensive Board Review',
        'slug' => 'chapter-mastery-board-review-substance-disruptive',
        'description' => 'Comprehensive synthesis of Substance-Related, Addictive, and Disruptive Disorders under DSM-5 criteria, and high-yield board pearls.',
        'overview' => "This capstone unit delivers an advanced synthesis of Substance-Related, Addictive, and Disruptive Disorders under DSM-5 criteria, consolidating core diagnostic formulations, duration thresholds, and high-yield board pearls for professional licensure examinations. Addictive and disruptive pathology represents heavily tested content across clinical examinations, demanding exact command over diagnostic criteria, neurobiological mechanisms, and differential boundaries.\n\nMastery requires commanding the clinical distinctions separating Substance Use Disorders, Alcohol Withdrawal Delirium (DTs), Wernicke-Korsakoff Syndrome, Opioid Overdose and Withdrawal, Stimulant Toxicity, Gambling Disorder, Oppositional Defiant Disorder (ODD), Conduct Disorder (CD), Pyromania, and Kleptomania. Furthermore, candidates must navigate DSM-5 structural refinements, including the unified 11-criteria SUD model, the elimination of the abuse/dependence dichotomy, the relocation of Gambling Disorder, the ODD-to-DMDD override rule, and the limited prosocial emotions specifier in CD.\n\nBy synthesizing clinical vignettes, diagnostic decision matrices, and evidence-based neurobiological mechanisms, this unit prepares students and clinicians for board examination excellence and compassionate clinical practice.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Mastery Summary of Core Addictive and Disruptive Diagnostic Rules:\n\n1. Substance Use Disorder: 11 criteria across 4 clusters (Impaired control, Social impairment, Risky use, Pharmacological). Severity: Mild (2-3), Moderate (4-5), Severe (6+) in 12 months. Prescription tolerance/withdrawal exemption.\n\n2. Alcohol Withdrawal: Tremors (6-12h), Hallucinosis (12-24h; clear sensorium), Seizures (24-48h), Delirium Tremens (48-96h; delirium + autonomic storm). Wernicke triad: Confusion, Ataxia, Ophthalmoplegia (thiamine deficiency).\n\n3. Opioid Toxicity & Overdose: Overdose triad: Pinpoint pupils (miosis), Respiratory depression (<8-10 bpm), Coma -> Naloxone rescue. Withdrawal: Yawning, rhinorrhea, lacrimation, piloerection, dilated pupils.\n\n4. Stimulants: Mydriasis (dilated pupils), tachycardia, hypertension, formication bugs. Withdrawal crash: Hypersomnia, hyperphagia.\n\n5. Gambling Disorder: Sole behavioral addiction in DSM-5; >= 4 of 9 criteria in 12 months; chasing losses is pathognomonic. Rule out Bipolar Mania.\n\n6. Oppositional Defiant Disorder: >= 4 symptoms across 3 clusters (irritable, defiant, vindictive) for >= 6 months with >= 1 NON-SIBLING. Frequency: >= 1x/wk for age 5+. DMDD overrides ODD.\n\n7. Conduct Disorder: >= 3 of 15 criteria across 4 domains (Aggression, Property destruction, Theft, Rule violations) in 12 months. Childhood onset (<10 yrs) predicts adult ASPD. 'With limited prosocial emotions' specifier (callous-unemotional).\n\n8. Pyromania & Kleptomania: Deliberate fire-setting / stealing unneeded objects driven by tension-pleasure-relief cycles WITHOUT external gain.",
        'specifiers' => "Review of essential specifiers: Severity tiers in SUD (Mild, Moderate, Severe); 'On maintenance therapy'; 'With perceptual disturbances'; Childhood vs. Adolescent onset in CD; 'With limited prosocial emotions' in CD.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Key Board Differential Pearls:\n• Patient meets 4 SUD criteria over 12 months -> Moderate Substance Use Disorder.\n• Tremors, fever, disorientation, crawling bugs 3 days after quitting drinking -> Delirium Tremens (48-96h).\n• Hallucinations 18 hours after quitting drinking with CLEAR SENSORIUM -> Alcoholic Hallucinosis (not DTs).\n• Comatose patient with pinpoint pupils and 4 breaths/min -> Opioid Overdose (Naloxone immediately).\n• Dilated pupils, sweating, 140 bpm, picking at skin bugs -> Stimulant Intoxication (Cocaine/Methamphetamine).\n• Returning to casino the next day to win back lost money -> Chasing losses -> Gambling Disorder.\n• Child argues with teachers and parents but NO cruelty or property destruction -> ODD.\n• Child tortures cats, breaks into homes, feels no remorse -> Conduct Disorder, With limited prosocial emotions.\n• Stealing baby bibs with plenty of money, preceded by tension and followed by relief -> Kleptomania.",
        'clinical_example' => "A comprehensive clinical review scenario: A board examination vignette describes a 15-year-old male who has been truant from school for eight months, was caught setting fire to a neighbor's shed, and has initiated four armed muggings with a knife. When interviewed, he smiles and states: 'I don't care about anyone, and I'd do it again.' Records confirm fire setting began at age 8.\n\nBoard Exam Question Formulation: What is the diagnosis, subtype, and specifier? The candidate identifies that meeting 4 criteria across property destruction, aggression, and rule violations establishes Conduct Disorder; onset before age 10 establishes Childhood-onset type; and persistent callousness, lack of remorse, and unconcern for others establishes the specifier: Conduct Disorder, Childhood-onset type, With limited prosocial emotions.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield board pearls summary: SUD = 11 criteria in 12 months (Mild: 2-3, Mod: 4-5, Severe: 6+). DTs = 48-96h post-alcohol cessation, delirium + fever. Wernicke = thiamine before glucose. Opioid triad = miosis, apnea, coma (Naloxone). Stimulants = mydriasis, tachycardia, formication. Gambling = 4 of 9 criteria, chasing losses. ODD = 4 symptoms, 6 months, non-sibling (DMDD overrides ODD). Conduct Disorder = 3 of 15 criteria, aggression/theft (childhood onset predicts ASPD; limited prosocial emotions). Kleptomania = unneeded objects, tension/relief.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Addictive & Disruptive Master Matrix', 'icon' => '🏛️', 'definition' => 'Comprehensive command of duration thresholds, symptom counts, and severity rules across all substance/disruptive entities.'],
            ['title' => 'The Medical Withdrawal Emergencies', 'icon' => '🚨', 'definition' => 'Commanding the diagnosis and acute medical management of Delirium Tremens, Wernicke encephalopathy, and Opioid overdose.'],
            ['title' => 'External Incentive Diagnostic Razor', 'icon' => '⚖️', 'definition' => 'Differentiating impulse-control disorders (Kleptomania, Pyromania) from criminal conduct motivated by financial or personal gain.'],
            ['title' => 'The Conduct-to-Antisocial Bridge', 'icon' => '🌉', 'definition' => 'Understanding Conduct Disorder as the mandatory developmental precursor to adult Antisocial Personality Disorder.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Substance Use Disorder (SUD)', 'definition' => 'A cluster of cognitive, behavioral, and physiological symptoms indicating continued substance use despite significant substance-related problems.'],
            ['term' => 'Delirium Tremens (DTs)', 'definition' => 'A severe, life-threatening alcohol withdrawal state characterized by autonomic hyperarousal, fever, disorientation, and hallucinations.'],
            ['term' => 'Limited Prosocial Emotions', 'definition' => 'A DSM-5 specifier for Conduct Disorder designating individuals with callous-unemotional traits, lack of empathy, and absence of remorse.'],
            ['term' => 'Naloxone Rescue', 'definition' => 'The emergency pharmacological reversal of opioid-induced respiratory arrest using a competitive mu-opioid receptor antagonist.']
        ]
